<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Bligh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1754">1754</a>–<a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Bligh was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> officer remembered for the mutiny on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hms-bounty">HMS Bounty</a> in 1789, after which he sailed some 6,700 kilometres to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a> in an open boat. On a second voyage he brought breadfruit from Tahiti to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> in 1793. As governor of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a> from 1806 he was deposed by officers of the New South Wales Corps in the Rum Rebellion of 1808.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-bligh.jpg" alt="William Bligh" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Huey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WilliamBligh.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bligh was born on 9 September 1754, though it is not clear where. He was baptised on 4 October 1754 at St Andrew's Church in Plymouth, where his father, Francis, served as a customs officer, and his ancestral home of Tinten Manor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> is also a possibility. His mother, Jane Pearce, née Balsam, was a widow. He was signed for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> at the age of seven, a common way of recording sea experience for a commission, and in September 1771 he was transferred to HMS Crescent, where he stayed three years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bligh first came to notice as Master of Resolution under Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a>, who praised him on the voyage that proved Cook's last. In 1787 he took command of HMAV Bounty to carry breadfruit trees from Tahiti to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, where the plant was wanted as food for enslaved Africans on British plantations. On 28 April 1789 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fletcher-christian">Fletcher Christian</a>, a master's mate who had been Bligh's friend, led a mutiny. Bligh and eighteen loyalists were set adrift in the open launch, and after a voyage of more than 3,500 nautical miles they reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kupang">Kupang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a>.</p><p>The causes are debated: some sources describe Bligh as a tyrant, others argue he was no worse than the average captain and that the crew were drawn back to life in Tahiti. He was honourably acquitted at the court-martial in October 1790 and published a narrative of the mutiny. From 1791 to 1793 he commanded HMS Providence on a second breadfruit voyage, which succeeded as a transport but not as a food, since most of the enslaved people refused it.</p><p>The ackee, Blighia sapida, is named for him. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-camperdown">Battle of Camperdown</a> on 11 October 1797 his ship Director captured the Batavian flagship Vrijheid, and he received the Naval Gold Medal. He was a captain whose crews took part in the mutinies at Spithead and the Nore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bligh became governor of New South Wales in 1806 and moved against the rum trade of the New South Wales Corps. His confrontational style angered settlers and officials, among them John Macarthur, and on 26 January 1808 the Corps arrested him in the Rum Rebellion, an act the Foreign Office later declared illegal. Major Johnston was cashiered after a court martial, and Bligh was promoted to rear admiral and in 1814 to vice-admiral, though he never again held an important command. He surveyed Dublin Bay and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 7 December 1817.</p><p>His logbooks of the mutiny were inscribed on the UNESCO Australian Memory of the World register in 2021, and the films of 1935 and 1962 largely perpetuate the image of him as a tyrant, while that of 1984 attempts a more neutral portrayal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Bligh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>William Bligh</b>’s HMS Bounty anchored in Matavai Bay in October 1788, and after the mutiny of April 1789 sixteen of its men returned to live on Tahiti.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tahiti">Kingdom of Tahiti · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mutineers seized HMS Bounty and set Captain <b>William Bligh</b> adrift with 18 men in an open launch.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-28">28 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Early New South Wales was a military-run penal colony, troubled by famine and by the officers' rum trade that ended in the deposition of Governor <b>William Bligh</b> in 1808.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-new-south-wales">Colony of New South Wales</a></li><li><blockquote>The botanic garden founded in 1765 is among the oldest in the Americas and received <b>William Bligh's</b> breadfruit in 1793.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-saint-vincent">Colony of Saint Vincent</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Plymouth, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.37,-4.14&amp;year=1794" aria-label="Plymouth on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of William Bligh"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4774 5813h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4774 5813h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.37,-4.14&amp;year=1794">What was here in 1794? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-journeys"><p class="enc-article-foot enc-journey"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/journeys?j=bounty">On the globe, stop by stop: HMS Bounty: Tahiti, the Mutiny and the Two Ways Home &rarr;</a></p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Just before sun-rising, Mr. Christian, with the master at arms, gunner's mate, and Thomas Burket, seaman, came into my cabin while I was asleep, and seizing me, tied my hands with a cord behind my back…&#8221;</p><footer>A Narrative of the Mutiny on Board His Majesty's Ship Bounty (1790), 28 April 1789 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20337/pg20337-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I now unhappily saw that nothing could be done to effect the recovery of the ship: there was no one to assist me, and every endeavour on my part was answered with threats of death.&#8221;</p><footer>A Narrative of the Mutiny on Board His Majesty's Ship Bounty (1790), 28 April 1789 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20337/pg20337-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…they imagined it in their power to fix themselves in the midst of plenty, on the finest island in the world, where they need not labour, and where the allurements of dissipation are beyond any thing that can be conceived.&#8221;</p><footer>A Narrative of the Mutiny on Board His Majesty's Ship Bounty (1790), on the mutineers' motives &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20337/pg20337-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
